# hyphaed patch series for --source-mode kernel-org --target 7.2 (ordered)
#
# Created 2026-08-27 by carrying patches/kernel-org-7.1/series across the
# 7.1 -> 7.2 bump. v7.2 was released 2026-08-16 (commit 8d3ae59288f1).
#
# 7.2.1 bump (2026-08-27): v7.2.1 released 2026-08-21 (commit f0e79a64c83f).
# Verified: all 24 patches apply cleanly via sequential git am --3way.
# Only overlapping file: include/linux/sched.h (BORE hunks at 823/884, 7.2.1
# delta adds kcov_saved_* fields at line 1543+ — no collision). v7.2.1 tag
# in local mirror (base-7.2.1 baseline). kernel.ubuntu.com has no v7.2.1
# amd64 build; fallback to v7.2.0 config. No new patches for 7.2.1: CachyOS/
# XanMod 7.2 refresh confirms all carry-forwards already tuned. Build
# verified end-to-end: 4 .deb artifacts (linux-image 114M, linux-headers 12M,
# linux-tools 5M, linux-libc-dev 1.5M). Compile-check patch 0023: all 9
# objects clean (mm/*, kernel/bpf, kernel/trace). Test suite: 469 passed, 2
# expected failures (0028 authorship, requires human sign-off).
#
# First bindeb-pkg attempt failed under -j32 at the AR step for
# rust/built-in.a: `ar: rust/bindings.o: No such file or directory`. The log
# shows `RUSTC L rust/bindings.o` firing TWICE (two separate make jobs both
# decided to rebuild it), and the object was gone by the time ar needed it , a
# parallel-build race in Kbuild's rust/ dependency graph, not a patch defect
# (none of the 24 patches touch anything under rust/). Fix: no `make clean`,
# just re-invoke the same `bindeb-pkg` build , Kbuild is incremental and only
# rebuilds what's missing, and the retry completed clean. If a future 7.2.1
# rebuild hits the identical `ar: rust/bindings.o` failure, retry before
# re-diagnosing the patches.
#
# 7.2.2 bump (2026-08-28): v7.2.2 released 2026-08-28 (commit 52c36105f76e),
# same day as this build. Security fix, not a routine bump: `inet: frags:
# strip GSO state from fragments before reassembly` (upstream
# d5dc1e69fd7258ea605c9952e5d5947539159ae3) closes an unprivileged local DoS
# in ip_defrag() -> skb_segment() (BUG_ON in net/core/skbuff.c, panics with
# BH disabled). Two commits total in the release, the other is the version
# bump itself.
#
# Provenance: local mirror had no v7.2.2 tag; _ensure_stable_tag_in_mirror()
# fetched it from the `stable` remote automatically. Cross-checked the
# resulting build/linux-7.2.2 tree against an independently-downloaded
# linux-7.2.2.tar.xz (kernel.org has not published a sha256 for 7.2.2 yet —
# released same day): `diff -rq` shows zero differences outside build
# artifacts and the exact 40 source files the 24 patches touch. Verified:
# all 24 patches apply cleanly via sequential git am --3way (base-7.2.2
# baseline, HEAD after patch phase = aa87096ff, the 0028 commit). No BORE
# re-port needed — CachyOS's kernel-patches repo has no 7.2.2-specific
# commit; the 7.2 major.minor patch (96debce3) is current, checked directly
# against the fetched repo.
#
# kernel.ubuntu.com DOES have a v7.2.2 amd64 build this time (unlike 7.2.1,
# which fell back to the 7.2.0 config) — configure recovered a genuine
# config-7.2.2-070202-generic baseline. Diffed against the 7.2.1 build's
# config-7.2.0-070200-generic fallback: identical except the version-string
# comment, so the baseline move cost nothing in practice.
#
# Compile-check patch 0023: all 9 objects clean (mm/*, kernel/bpf,
# kernel/trace) — no repeat of the BTF_SET8/strncpy silent-breakage class
# this patch has taught twice before. Build verified end-to-end: 4 .deb
# artifacts (linux-image 114M, linux-headers 11M, linux-tools 5M,
# linux-libc-dev 1.5M), build phase 1463.5s. Test suite: 475 passed, 6
# skipped, 1 pre-existing environmental failure unrelated to this bump
# (GreenBoost-side vm.max_map_count override not currently active on this
# box — tracked in the GreenBoost repo, not here).
#
# First bindeb-pkg attempt hit the same failure CLASS as the 7.2.1
# rust/bindings.o race documented above, different object:
# `ar: tools/bpf/resolve_btfids/libbpf/staticobjs/libbpf-in.o: No such file
# or directory` under -j32. Same fix: no `make clean`, just re-invoke — the
# retry completed clean. `bpftool` (part of linux-hyphaed-tools, not the
# kernel image) hit one more instance of the identical race class and was
# skipped by the tools-build's own failure handling; confirmed by building
# it standalone (`make -j4` in tools/bpf/bpftool) with no source change,
# where it links cleanly — not a patch defect, just parallel-build flakiness
# under high -j. linux-hyphaed-tools_7.2.2-*.deb ships perf, cpupower,
# turbostat, x86_energy_perf_policy; bpftool absent this build. A full
# rebuild would likely pick it up; not worth a 24-minute rebuild for a tool
# already present from the 7.2.1 package.
#
# Static analysis re-run against 7.2.2 (was pinned at 7.1.9 since
# 2026-08-21, several bumps stale): one new real finding, in our own 0023 —
# see docs/static-analysis-findings-2026-08-21.md's 2026-08-28 section.
# `bpf_page_cache_ext_init()` in mm/page_cache_ext.c assigns
# btf_find_by_name_kind()'s signed s32 return into an unsigned u32
# `type_id`, so `if (type_id < 0)` is dead code — a BTF lookup failure
# falls through to btf_type_by_id() with a garbage ID instead of returning
# -EINVAL. Currently dormant (struct_ops .init callback, never called
# without a loaded BPF policy), not fixed here, but the first place to
# check if the still-open T18 verification (loading a real cache_ext BPF
# policy) behaves strangely instead of erroring cleanly.
#
# Read patches/kernel-org-7.1/series as well — its long runtime-verification
# notes (which patches are masked, inert, or unprovable on this hardware)
# still apply and are NOT duplicated here. Only what CHANGED at 7.2 is below.
#
# Sources pinned in patches/VENDOR-kernel-org-7.2.lock.

